============================================================================================
PART 1: g(t) := sin(log(1+t)) is bounded and slowly-oscillating
(relative-step sense) -- verified symbolically/analytically
============================================================================================
g(t) = sin(log(t + 1))
|g(t)| <= 1 for all t>=0: trivial (sin is bounded).  (H-bdd) HOLDS.

g(y(1+delta_frac)) - g(y), series in delta_frac (fixed y), to O(delta_frac^2):
δ_frac⋅y⋅cos(log(y + 1))
────────────────────────
         y + 1          

leading coefficient (d/d(delta_frac) at 0) as y->infinity: does NOT
simplify to a constant (it is cos(log(1+y)) * y/(1+y), itself bounded
by 1 in absolute value for ALL y) -- so |g(s)-g(y)| <= delta_frac + o(delta_frac)
UNIFORMLY in y, for ALL y>=0 (no exceptional points, unlike a
piecewise-constant construction with boundaries). This IS the exact
(H-osc) relative-step condition, genuinely satisfied for ALL y, not
just asymptotically or on a subsequence.

Numerical confirmation at several y, delta_frac=0.01,0.05,0.1:
  y=    10: delta=0.01: |g(s)-g(y)|=0.00669 (<=delta? YES) | delta=0.05: |g(s)-g(y)|=0.03337 (<=delta? YES) | delta=0.1: |g(s)-g(y)|=0.06652 (<=delta? YES)
  y=   100: delta=0.01: |g(s)-g(y)|=0.00091 (<=delta? YES) | delta=0.05: |g(s)-g(y)|=0.00353 (<=delta? YES) | delta=0.1: |g(s)-g(y)|=0.00472 (<=delta? YES)
  y=  1000: delta=0.01: |g(s)-g(y)|=0.00803 (<=delta? YES) | delta=0.05: |g(s)-g(y)|=0.03880 (<=delta? YES) | delta=0.1: |g(s)-g(y)|=0.07442 (<=delta? YES)
  y= 10000: delta=0.01: |g(s)-g(y)|=0.00973 (<=delta? YES) | delta=0.05: |g(s)-g(y)|=0.04790 (<=delta? YES) | delta=0.1: |g(s)-g(y)|=0.09394 (<=delta? YES)

============================================================================================
PART 2: g's own Cesaro mean, (1/y) int_0^y g(t) dt, computed EXACTLY
============================================================================================
Substituting t=e^u-1 (dt=e^u du): int sin(u)*e^u du = -sqrt(2)*exp(u)*cos(u + pi/4)/2

int_0^Y g(t) dt = -sqrt(2)*(Y + 1)*cos(log(Y + 1) + pi/4)/2 + 1/2

Cesaro mean (1/Y) int_0^Y g(t) dt = (-sqrt(2)*(Y + 1)*cos(log(Y + 1) + pi/4) + 1)/(2*Y)

[check] d/dY[int_0^Y g dt] - g(Y) = 0  (should be 0)
-> PASS: exact antiderivative confirmed by direct differentiation.

============================================================================================
PART 3: NEITHER g(Y) NOR its Cesaro mean converges as Y->infinity
(both oscillate with the SAME non-vanishing amplitude)
============================================================================================
Cesaro mean - (1/2)(sin(log(1+Y))-cos(log(1+Y)))  ->  AccumBounds(sqrt(2)*AccumBounds(-1/2, 1/2) - 1, sqrt(2)*AccumBounds(-1/2, 1/2) + 1)  as Y->inf
(i.e. the Cesaro mean is asymptotically (1/2)(sin(log(1+Y))-cos(log(1+Y))),
 an oscillation of amplitude sqrt(2)/2, NOT a convergent sequence.)

         Y         g(Y)    Cesaro mean
     1e+01     0.677014       0.827141
     1e+02    -0.995273      -0.448570
     1e+03     0.585559      -0.112148
     1e+04     0.212700       0.595018
     1e+05    -0.869127      -0.681860
     1e+06     0.948713       0.316289
     1e+07    -0.398731       0.259169
     1e+08    -0.415848      -0.662641

Both columns keep oscillating without settling, no matter how large Y
gets -- CONFIRMING that (H-bdd)+(H-osc) alone, without an independently
established (H-ces), do NOT imply convergence. The relative-step
oscillation bound this front derives for Phi (s01/s02) is therefore
NOT, by itself, sufficient input to the classical Tauberian theorem --
Cesaro convergence of A(y)/(x+y) must ALSO be established, separately,
and is NOT addressed by (i) or by the self-averaging identity (which
only says Phi_y(x) and A(y)/(x+y) differ by o(1) -- it does not, and
cannot, say anything about whether A(y)/(x+y) itself has a limit).
